# KillSwitch — REFERENCE ruleset template (for humans to read only).
#
# NOTE: this file is NOT loaded at runtime. The source of truth is the generator
# PFRulesetManager.makeRuleset(...) in Daemon/PFRulesetManager.swift. The daemon
# builds the ruleset in code rather than reading this file: the firewall runs in
# fail-closed mode, and depending on an external file would risk having no rules
# (no protection) if the file were missing.
#
# This shows the static shape so the structure is easy to understand. Dynamic parts
# the generator substitutes:
#   <servers> — addresses of allowed servers (/32);
#   the <lan> pass line — present only when the LAN toggle is on.

set block-policy drop
set skip on lo0

# Base: block all traffic in both directions (R1).
block in all
block out all

# IPv6 fully blocked, no exceptions — even inside the tunnel (R4).
block quick inet6 all

# Trust traffic inside any utun tunnel (R7, R11).
pass quick on utun all

# Allowed servers — dynamic /32 table (R8, R10).
table <servers> persist
pass out quick inet proto { tcp udp } from any to <servers>

# Local network — enabled by a toggle (R15).
table <lan> const { 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 169.254/16 }
# pass quick inet from any to <lan>   # enabled by the generator when LAN is on

# Preserve Apple's system anchors — AirDrop, sharing (R19).
anchor "com.apple/*"
